why 2 1/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs B–C–D–E–F by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 11 3/4 UNRESOLVED_NCT The tune repeats E over the D minor chord and falls to D: a suspension struck again instead of tied, resolving down by step.
why 13 3/4 DIRECT The tune falls C–A while the bass drops an octave within the same A chord. The chord does not change, only the bass's register, so there is nothing new to hide.
why 14 0 CROSSING The alto drops to A under the tenor's E for one beat, leaving room for the tune falling A–G–F. On the next beat it climbs to D, above the tenor again.
why 14 1/8 CROSSING The same crossing for one more eighth.
why 16 3/4 SIX_FOUR The ending is written out slowly, with a fermata on each chord: the ⁶₄ comes on the last beat and its V on the downbeat of the final bar.
why 17 0 CADENCE The melody ends on E, so Bach ends on the E major chord, which in A minor is V: a Phrygian ending, common in chorales.
why 17 0 LEAPS The alto walks down by step to B in the last chord. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
